  "answer_text": "Great question! Here's a detailed explanation of why nitrogen is used in plastic welding:\n\n---\n\n## Why Nitrogen is Used in Plastic Welding\n\n### 1. \ud83d\udee1\ufe0f It Acts as a Shielding Gas\nNitrogen gas is used when plastic welding because it acts as a shielding gas, protecting the weld area from contaminants in the air. It works much like shielding gases used when welding steel or aluminum with a MIG welder, preventing the plastic from oxidizing during the welding process.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \u26a0\ufe0f The Problem with Regular Air (Oxygen)\nAir is composed of 80% nitrogen and 20% oxygen. The oxygen in the air causes oxidation, burning the plastic during the welding process. This results in smoke, ash, and sparks, which weaken the weld and create contamination.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \u2705 Nitrogen Eliminates Oxidation\nWelding with nitrogen eliminates this issue by flushing out the oxygen from the weld zone. Without oxygen, the plastic melts cleanly without burning or oxidizing. This allows the melted plastics to fuse together with no contamination for a stronger, more durable weld.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83d\udcaa Produces a Stronger, Cleaner Weld\nA nitrogen hot air welder uses compressed nitrogen gas to eliminate oxygen from the weld area. The nitrogen acts as a shielding gas and allows for a contaminant-free weld with less smoke, which creates a stronger weld. Industry experts will tell you that you get a stronger and more durable repair using nitrogen plastic welders.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \ud83c\udf21\ufe0f Better Thermal Properties\nNitrogen also has thermodynamic properties that make it useful when welding plastics like ABS and polypropylene; its high heat capacity allows for faster cooling of the welded material without causing warping or cracking due to thermal shock.\n\n---\n\n### 6. \ud83d\udd04 Efficient Use of Gas\nThis type of welder can also switch to compressed air so that when preheating or cooling down the heating element, it does not waste the nitrogen.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\nIn short, nitrogen is used in plastic welding primarily because it is an **inert gas** that displaces oxygen, preventing the oxidation and burning that would otherwise weaken or contaminate the weld. The result is a cleaner, stronger, and more durable repair \u2014 especially important for materials like polypropylene, ABS, and polyethylene used in automotive parts.",
